&lt;p&gt;I found this image in an old french book of lettres. I was wondering if it could be desaturated and made into a font somehow. I do not have the resources or the know-how to do this. If you&amp;rsquo;re able to do this and don&amp;rsquo;t mind, I&amp;rsquo;d be very appreciative if you could send me over the .otf or .ttf file.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Sous Style Font ID - Sans and Serif Font ID??</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/102481/</link><pubDate>Tue, 23 Apr 2013 10:06:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/102481/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Hi Typophiles,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Wondering if you could help me out with IDing the fonts used on this site: &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20140715002430/http:/www.sousstyle.com/"&gt;Sous Style&lt;/a&gt;. I&amp;rsquo;ve also attached a screen shot.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It looks like they&amp;rsquo;re using a serif (in the CSS it specifies Baskerville, but I&amp;rsquo;ve not known Baskerville to look like that) and an unknown &amp;ldquo;Geometric Light&amp;rdquo; embedded typeface - but I&amp;rsquo;m having no luck identifying either.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks much!!&lt;br&gt;
Michelle&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="http://web.archive.org/web/20140715002430im_/http:/typophile.com/files/SousStyleSite_5854.jpg" alt=""&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Font Style</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/101960/</link><pubDate>Wed, 03 Apr 2013 06:35:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/101960/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Can anyone tell me what font style the initials JC are on the cuff link in this photo ??&lt;br&gt;
